WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.875
Atomic writes showed how related
changes become visible as a whole.

2
00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:08.728
Here, we ask how data goes away:
on its own, or by mistake.

3
00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:12.952
This is TTL and soft delete:
entries can expire on their own,

4
00:00:12.952 --> 00:00:17.016
and a deleted tree can be recovered
inside its retention window.

5
00:00:18.016 --> 00:00:20.433
A session should not live for ever.

6
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.887
Write it with a time to live,
here thirty minutes.

7
00:00:24.237 --> 00:00:28.067
The silo turns that duration
into an absolute expiry time,

8
00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:31.302
so a client
with a skewed clock does not move it.

9
00:00:31.652 --> 00:00:35.368
Once that time passes,
the entry is gone from every read.

10
00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:40.779
A get returns nothing, an existence check
says false, and scans and counts skip it.

11
00:00:41.779 --> 00:00:44.090
Expiry follows the write that wins.

12
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.864
A later write replaces the entry as usual,

13
00:00:46.864 --> 00:00:51.289
and a winning plain write
without a time to live clears the expiry.

14
00:00:51.639 --> 00:00:56.230
On a CRDT entry,
expiries merge by taking the later one,

15
00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.018
so a refresh only ever extends it.

16
00:00:59.368 --> 00:01:02.474
An expired entry is
not deleted straight away.

17
00:01:02.474 --> 00:01:06.053
Compaction reaps it after
the tombstone grace period,

18
00:01:06.053 --> 00:01:08.686
so a lagging peer cannot bring it back.

19
00:01:09.686 --> 00:01:12.764
Deleting a whole tree is a soft delete.

20
00:01:12.764 --> 00:01:17.974
The tree becomes inaccessible at once:
its reads and writes throw.

21
00:01:18.324 --> 00:01:20.035
But its data is not gone.

22
00:01:20.035 --> 00:01:24.416
It is kept for a retention window,
seventy-two hours by default,

23
00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:25.785
before it is purged.

24
00:01:26.785 --> 00:01:30.756
Inside that window, recovering
the tree makes it accessible again,

25
00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:33.404
with all the data it
held before the delete.

26
00:01:33.754 --> 00:01:35.692
Once the purge has started,

27
00:01:35.692 --> 00:01:40.358
it is too late; and once the purge
is complete, the data is gone.

28
00:01:42.158 --> 00:01:44.212
Next on Build: Going durable.

29
00:01:44.212 --> 00:01:48.320
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
